<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"> <head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Interior Doors &amp; Closet Build-Outs, Central MA | MCB</title><meta name="description" content="Interior doors and closets in Central MA: typical costs per door, pre-hung vs slab, solid vs hollow core, pocket doors and closet systems for old homes."><link rel="canonical" href="https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry/interior-doors-and-closets"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Interior Doors &amp; Closet Build-Outs, Central MA | MCB"><meta property="og:description" content="Interior doors and closets in Central MA: typical costs per door, pre-hung vs slab, solid vs hollow core, pocket doors and closet systems for old homes."><meta property="og:url" content="https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry/interior-doors-and-closets"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Maverick City Builders"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Manrope:wght@200;300;400;500;600;700&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"GeneralContractor","@id":"https://mcitybuilders.com/#business","name":"Maverick City Builders","url":"https://mcitybuilders.com","telephone":"(508) 656-7436","email":"douglas@mcitybuilders.com","address":{"@type":"PostalAddress","streetAddress":"1171 Main St","addressLocality":"Lancaster","addressRegion":"MA","postalCode":"01523","addressCountry":"US"},"geo":{"@type":"GeoCoordinates","latitude":42.4556,"longitude":-71.6731},"sameAs":["https://www.facebook.com/mcitybuilders","https://www.instagram.com/mcitybuilders"]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Carpentry","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Interior Doors and Closets","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry/interior-doors-and-closets"}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","serviceType":"Interior Doors and Closets","url":"https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry/interior-doors-and-closets","provider":{"@id":"https://mcitybuilders.com/#business"},"areaServed":{"@type":"AdministrativeArea","name":"Central Massachusetts"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I buy pre-hung doors or just new slabs?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Pre-hung is usually faster and more reliable when the old jambs are worn, out of square or you are changing casing anyway. A slab makes sense when the existing jambs and trim are worth keeping, which is common in older homes with original casing you don't want to disturb."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a solid-core door worth the extra cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For bedrooms, bathrooms and home offices, usually yes. Solid-core doors block noise better, feel heavier and close with a firm sound. For closets and linen cabinets, hollow-core is fine and saves money."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do my doors stick in summer and gap in winter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Wood doors and jambs take on moisture in humid months and release it in dry, heated months. A door fitted too tight in January binds in July. Sealing all six sides of a door and fitting it with the right gaps helps a lot. Sticking can also be the house settling, which is a framing issue, not a door issue."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you put in a pocket door in an existing wall?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, but the wall has to be opened, it cannot hold plumbing, ductwork or much wiring in the pocket side, and if it is a bearing wall the header needs to be sized for the wider opening. We check before we price it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What door goes between the garage and the house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The code calls for a fire-resistant door there, such as solid wood or solid or honeycomb-core steel at least 1-3/8 inches thick, or a 20-minute fire-rated door. Some towns also look for a self-closing device. Your building department confirms what applies."}}]}</script><!-- First-touch attribution. Runs before anything else so a click-id is never
